From ef2423ebe1eb2d0b080419d53846205f7d6ea781 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 08:32:59 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] cursor: bill tool-loop segments from a run ledger so cached
 prompts show up
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Cursor reports usage exactly once per Agent run, in the TurnEnded that closes
it, and those counters are cumulative over every segment the run produced. One
gateway request drives one segment, so an agentic tool loop finished almost
every request with no usage at all: the response carried no usage chunk, the
statistics store fell back to a char/4 estimate of the whole prompt with zero
cached tokens, and the run's closing request then reported the entire run
again. Production logs show this directly — 66 of the 103 requests served in
the last hour ended on finish_reason=tool_calls with no usage anywhere in the
response, which is what surfaces as five-figure prompts with no cache.

The session now keeps a ledger for the run. Segments that end without a report
are billed from an estimate that charges only the growth beyond the prefix the
run already sent, counting the rest as a cache read; the cumulative report is
then reduced by whatever those estimates covered, so a run still totals
Cursor's own numbers. Every segment_end is preceded by a usage report, so the
tool_calls responses that make up an agent loop stop looking uncached.

On the wire prompt_tokens is now the whole prompt with cached_tokens a subset,
which is what the OpenAI shape means and what the Claude translator needs to
recover cache_read_input_tokens. Forwarding Cursor's disjoint input_tokens as
prompt_tokens hid the cached prefix entirely and let cached_tokens exceed the
prompt it was supposedly part of.

Co-authored-by: Apologetic2022 <Apologetic2022@users.noreply.github.com>
